## 2026-06-24 — interned IntMap trie (PathItem -> Int; evalI)

Added src/main/scala/IntTrie.scala:
- Interner: global, O(1) PathItem<->Int (ConcurrentHashMap + ArrayList); ids stable per process.
- ITrie(terminal, IntMap[ITrie]): trie keyed by interned ints. Ring ops use IntMap's callback
  merges — union = unionWith(recursive union), intersection = intersectionWith(recursive
  intersection); subtraction/restriction/composition/wrap/unwrap/tails/joinAll/meetAll analogous.
- evalI: direct evaluator over ITrie. Path constants interned at construction; the operations
  only combine ints — NO PathItem is created during evaluation. Grounded fns re-intern at the
  SpaceValue boundary. Includes short-circuiting + Literal->ITrie memo (as evalT).
Tests (TrieTest.scala): ITrieOps (ops + join/meet vs reference over random spaces) and
ITrieEval (evalI == eval on aunt, transitive, GoL, pure n-queens, pure sliding). All green.

Benchmarks (BENCHMARKS.md): evalI vs eval (Set) and vs evalT (TreeMap trie):
  vs reference: datalog 31.8x, aunt 41.7x, temperature 944x, GoL 7.7x, sliding 4.7x, n-queens
  18.2x; geomean ~16x across the six pure domains. vs TreeMap trie: 1.1-2.4x (geomean ~1.5x).
  meet-all 3.5-16.3x over pairwise reduce on the interned trie.
The interned IntMap trie is the most performant evaluator; interning is global/constant-time.

