## 2026-06-25 (cont.) — compile-time budgeting + per-pass timing

User: "A supercompiler should be bounded in how much time it spends compilation as opposed to
runtime. And optimization passes should have their time reported, too."

Added (MORKL.scala):
- Deadline — a wall-clock bound (Deadline.never / Deadline.inMillis); fixed-point passes poll it
  and stop GRACEFULLY (each pass is semantics-preserving, so the best graph reached is correct).
- Profiler — accounts time + call-count per named pass; Profiler.off is a zero-overhead sink.
- optimize(g, budget, prof): the push_out/optimize_sharing fixpoint now (a) stops when `budget`
  expires (sound early-out) and (b) times each pass into `prof`. all_forever/inlineCalls take a
  budget too. Defaults (never/off) keep every existing caller unchanged.

Supercompiler:
- SC.Config gains `compileBudgetMs` (default ∞; the count caps maxNodes/maxDepth/maxReduce still
  bound it). State carries a Deadline; scCall throws SC.CompileBudgetExceeded when it expires.
- run() catches it and FALLS BACK to interpreting the original program — env = `materialize(conf,
  defs)` (every routine reachable from conf) — marked `converged = false`. So compilation is
  time-bounded and degrades gracefully instead of hanging or crashing.
- SCReport gains `compileMillis` + `phaseMillis` (supercompile, transpile, push_out,
  optimize_sharing) and a `timing` renderer; compileCall threads one shared deadline through the
  driver AND the optimizer so TOTAL compile time stays within the budget.

GraphBench now emits a second table, "Compile time per pass (executor-ready build)", separating
one-time compile cost from per-run time (compile/run ratio).  It makes the point concrete:
temperature 16384 spends ~317 ms compiling (mostly transpiling a 16k-literal table) for a ~0.06 ms
run — compile/run ≈ 5600x — exactly the case a budget must bound; aunt/datalog are ≈1x or below.

BUG found and fixed while wiring this up: a single-line Scala 3 catch arm
  `catch case X => stmt1; stmt2; stmt3`
does NOT put stmt2/stmt3 in the arm — they leak into the enclosing block and run UNCONDITIONALLY.
So `run`'s fallback (`st.converged = false; Residual(conf, materialize(...))`) executed on EVERY
compile, making every SC run fall back to the un-optimized program with converged=false. This
silently broke all specialization (aunt/temperature/SCFacade "X specialized away" tests: the
residual was the original routine, not the specialized one). Fixed by giving the catch arm an
explicit multi-line block. Lesson: never chain `;` statements on a one-line catch/case arm.

Suite (excl. Slow): 144 passing / 0 failing (was 142; +2 SC tests: budget fallback is sound, and
optimization-pass times are reported).

