10. Properly fix push_out (do NOT depower it) — it is a primary perf driver.
11. Properly solve CSE incl. SUBGRAPH sharing via structural hashing — primary perf driver.

Order: structural hashing (foundational) -> Fixpoint first-class -> push_out hardening (drop
catch) -> CSE+subgraph sharing -> SCC lowerer -> deadline threading + finite default -> stats &
reporting -> grounded audit -> property tests.  Run tests after each step; iterate to publishable.

## step: structural-hash convergence + drop the catch (items 8, 9)
- optimize no longer `catch _: Throwable => in` / wellFormed-reverts; it trusts the passes (a bad
  graph is now a surfaced bug, not a hidden revert).  Convergence compares a 64-bit structuralHash
  (ops/constants/kinds/coords/nesting) instead of `g.show` strings.  wellFormed kept for tests.
- Full non-Slow suite: 144/0 — confirms push_out + optimize_sharing are robust WITHOUT the net.

## step: proper CSE incl. subgraph sharing (item 11)  [DONE]
- Rewrote optimize_sharing as value-numbering hash-consing (O(n) via an intern table), replacing the
  O(n^2) `find`-based scan.  Keys are EXACT (length-prefixed constant; lossy hashing is unsound for
  CSE — a collision merges different values).  Visibility is scoped (only redirect to an ancestor or
  an earlier same-scope node, never a sibling).  Subgraphs now share: key = SG|rootOp|<rootVNs>|
  <bodyVNs>|constant, so two structurally identical iterations deduplicate (new test proves 2->1).
- BUG surfaced by removing the catch + by subgraph handling: an Extract is a BINDING, not a value;
  a Fixpoint/Iteration body's ExtractSpaceMention("edges") (the loop var) was being CSE-merged with
  the ancestor's ExtractSpaceMention("edges") (the initial), so the body computed next(initial) not
  next(cur) -> datalog optimize gave a wrong (extra-edge) closure.  Fix: Extract keys are scope-LEVEL
  discriminated and Extracts are never redirected; same-level Extracts of identical sibling subgraphs
  still share a VN so subgraph dedup keeps working.
- Result node of every scope is PINNED (always emitted) so the executor's `.last` stays the true
  output even if it duplicates an earlier node.
- Full non-Slow suite: 145/0 (incl. new subgraph-CSE test).

