[2026-06-27] execZ on the LONG benchmarks (RecursionLoweringBench; median of 5, single-eval times).
RECURSION-lowered (Fixpoint materialized via the evalI "call"; only the outer local ops fuse):
  mutual f(x)=x∪g(x("h")): depth10 eval 2.11 / evalI 0.49 / execT 0.36 / execZ 0.25 ; depth40 8.19/0.21/0.75/0.47
  transitive closure chain: N=48 eval 40.73 / evalI 0.86 / execT 0.74 / execZ 0.63
  -> execZ ~ evalI (often a touch faster); recursion dominates and is shared via the call.
DEEP LOCAL-algebra (depth-N nesting of ∪/∩/\ over large tries — execZ's fusion target):
  depth12 trie1200 |out|249 : eval 0.19 / evalI 0.18 / execT 0.15 / execZ 0.45  (execZ/evalI 2.51x, execZ/eval 2.43x)
  depth24 trie2000 |out|777 : eval 1.13 / evalI 0.28 / execT 0.30 / execZ 0.94  (execZ/evalI 3.40x, execZ/eval 0.83x)
  depth40 trie3000 |out| 45 : eval 1.95 / evalI 0.40 / execT 0.40 / execZ 1.31  (execZ/evalI 3.30x, execZ/eval 0.67x)
FINDING (honest): execZ is correct everywhere (asserted == eval at every size). It BEATS the Set-based
reference eval on deep local algebra (up to ~1.5x at depth40), but is ~2.5-3.4x SLOWER than the optimized
trie interpreter evalI there. Reason: the ITrie ops are already near-optimal BULK Patricia (IntMap)
merges with eq short-circuits; the zipper's deforestation (no intermediate tries) does NOT beat them
because materialize decomposes those same merges node-by-node and allocates a zipper wrapper per node —
the per-node abstraction overhead exceeds the saved intermediate materialization (which is cheap to build
and re-walk natively). On recursion-lowered programs execZ ~ evalI (recursion is the shared cost).
TAKEAWAY: the zipper paradigm's payoff is the UNIFIED single-traversal MODEL (declarative paths + calls),
not raw speed in this interpreted form — exactly the structure the planned lowering needs (choose what to
materialize; compile the per-node movements away into transpiled raw-iteration Rust, where the wrapper
overhead vanishes). As an interpreted executor it is correct and competitive on recursion, slower than the
bulk-merge evalI on pure local algebra.

[2026-06-27] execZ at SCALE — large datasets + the node-sharing study (ZipperScaleBench).
Operands pre-interned ONCE and fed via Mention/ic, so timing isolates the OP (both executors get O(1)
operand access; the asymptotics are not masked by an unavoidable O(|input|) interning both would pay).

(A) FLAT set-algebra, |A|=|B|=30000 deep paths (depth 12, alphabet 4), at 1%/50%/90% node sharing:
  execZ/evalI ratio (full materialization, so same work; execZ pays a virtual-cursor wrapper per node):
    1% : ∪ 1.36x  ∩ 10.39x  \ 2.70x     50% : ∪ 1.08x  ∩ 2.30x  \ 3.26x     90% : ∪ 5.10x  ∩ 1.41x  \ 2.09x
  -> constant-factor OVERHEAD (1.1–10x). evalI's bulk Patricia merges + eq short-circuit beat node-by-node
     materialization. Worst is ∩ at 1% sharing (tiny result but small alphabet ⇒ both tries densely cover
     all upper-level keys, so the intersection descends deep before pruning, and execZ has no eq/identity
     short-circuit on shared subtries — a known, fixable constant). This is the "little overhead" regime.

(B) ASYMPTOTIC regime — selective (A∪B)∩C, C⊆A fixed-small (400 paths) & localized, GROW |A|=|B|:
    |A|=|B|=10000 (98k nodes) : evalI 0.41  execZ 0.79  -> execZ 1.9x slower  (overhead dominates; small)
    |A|=|B|=40000 (325k nodes): evalI 1.32  execZ 0.13  -> execZ 9.9x FASTER
    |A|=|B|=160000(1.04M nodes): evalI 6.92 execZ 0.26  -> execZ 26.6x FASTER
  -> THE WIN. As input grows 16x, evalI grows ~17x (∝ input nodes) while execZ stays ~flat (∝ result=400).
     The fused zipper NEVER materializes the parts of A∪B the outer ∩ discards; eager evalI builds all of
     A∪B first. Speedup grows without bound — asymptotically better, exactly as expected. Crossover ~20–40k.

(B2) COMPOSITION & COMBINATIONS at 1%/50%/90% (|A|=|B|=30000), execZ/evalI:
    A∘small (240k out): 5.03x / 5.55x / 5.87x   -> full-splice materialization: constant overhead
    (A∪B)∩C [selective]: 0.23x / 0.16x / 0.16x  -> pruning WIN robust across ALL sharing levels (4–6x faster)
    A\(A∩B)            : 4.09x / 1.16x / 2.47x   -> full materialization: overhead
    (A∩B)∪(A\B) (=A)   : 2.19x / 2.14x / 3.07x   -> full materialization: overhead

(C) DATALOG transitive closure (chain N) — control-flow, execZ routes via the evalI "call":
    N=32 |TC|496 : eval 10.06 evalI 0.88 execZ 0.70 ; N=64 |TC|2016: eval 93.15 evalI 1.52 execZ 1.50
    N=96 |TC|4560: evalI 4.53 execZ 4.54   -> execZ ≈ evalI; both ~100x faster than the Set reference.
(D) AUNT query, synthetic genealogy: 120 ppl (443 facts, 81 aunts) eval 16.08 evalI 0.93 execZ 0.67 ;
    600 ppl (2388 facts, 698 aunts) eval 310.70 evalI 0.87 execZ 0.83  -> execZ ≈ evalI, ~370x over eval.
  Correctness gated at every size: execZ == evalI == eval.

SUMMARY: the zipper paradigm meets the expectation — a small constant overhead when the whole result must
be materialized (flat algebra, composition), but ASYMPTOTICALLY better (unbounded speedup, up to 26x here)
whenever an outer operator prunes a large inner sub-expression, because the single fused traversal never
builds the discarded structure. The only flat-regime wart is the missing eq/identity short-circuit on
shared subtries (the ∩-at-1% 10x) — a constant-factor fix, not asymptotic.

[2026-06-27] Referential-identity short-circuit + three-way intersection (ZipperScaleBench E/F).
Zipper.scala: added O(1) smart constructors union/intersection/subtraction with sameSpace(a,b) =
(a eq b) || (both Lit over the same trie object). x∪x=x and x∩x=x INSTANT-ACCEPT (return a, no descent);
x\x=∅ INSTANT-PRUNE. Routed children/descend/Composition and transpileZ through them, so a re-occurring
(eq) branch is accepted/pruned, never re-descended — a pure pointer test, preserves the constant-time
movement guarantee. Semantics unchanged (identity laws), so all prior correctness still holds.
  Verified: x∩x / x∪x / x\x correct; A\A over an 80000-path trie materializes in 0.0006 ms (O(1)).

THREE-WAY A∩B∩C — fused single traversal vs eager PAIRWISE (build A∩B, then ∩C). A,B overlap heavily
(A∩B ∝ N, off-prefix) while C is localized under a fixed prefix & fixed-small, so A∩B∩C = 300 (fixed):
    |c12|   |A∩B|  |A∩B∩C| |  pairwise   execZ | speedup
     2000    2300     300  |    0.17     0.18  |   1.0x   (parity; intermediate still tiny)
    16000   16300     300  |    2.65     0.07  |  35.9x
    64000   64300     300  |   22.10     0.07  | 323.0x
  -> execZ is FLAT (0.07 ms ∝ result) while eager pairwise grows ∝ |A∩B| (the intermediate it must build).
     The fused intersection prunes to the localized triple-common corner at the root and NEVER materializes
     the large A∩B-only mass. Asymptotically faster than pairwise, unbounded speedup — as predicted.
  NOTE: when the surviving triple-overlap is NOT localized (spread randomly over a small/medium alphabet),
     all three tries are dense near the root, so the intersection must descend the dense upper region before
     it can prune — there execZ tracks |A∩B| and the win is only a constant factor. The asymptotic win needs
     the outer operator to prune early (selective/localized survivor) — exactly the query-shaped workloads.

