2026-07-13  Plan: constraint-based (relational) size analysis
================================================================================
design_size_constraints.md written.  Motivation: the interval analysis is
non-relational — x \/ (x /\ y) over-estimates (true |x|) and x /\ (x /\ y)
under-estimates (true |x /\ y|) because shared subterms are treated as
independent.  Plan: hash-consed term DAG (shared subterms = one variable
n(t), eps(t); hd derived), Horn-saturated subset (⊑) and disjointness facts,
per-constructor cardinality constraints (inclusion-exclusion, the certified
restriction/raffination partition, disjoint-cylinder unions, keyed-iteration
sums), consumed by two tiers: (1) in-process bound propagation to a unique
fixpoint (monotone integer narrowing — terminating, budgeted, the default),
(2) z3 Optimize (nuZ) min/max of n(root) as the exact oracle + on-demand API,
reusing the proofs-pipeline plumbing.  Both motivating examples already close
at milestone M1 via pure ⊑-saturation (lub/glb closure rules), before any
inclusion-exclusion.  Every constraint schema to be certified in proofs/laws
like the rewrite laws.  Five milestones; evaluation = SizeBoundsReport deltas
(seed vs relational vs exact columns).
--- 15-puzzle obligation: full forensics ------------------------------------------
Timeline from the run logs: exactly ONE genuine pass (Jul 12, equiv4.log,
486.237s — z3 discharged the single REAL obligation in the file within its
240s budget) and three consistent Jul 13 failures (laws tree, size-analysis
tree, idle machine) on a BYTE-IDENTICAL obligation file with the SAME z3
binary (4.15.4, installed Jun 3).  The file bundles 3 candidate pairs: 2
law-justified + 1 real goal, so the justification fast-path is NOT the
difference.  Standalone measurements: z3 times out at 900s with pathological
KERNEL time (512s system / 84s user), and again after stopping the 3.9GB
bloop daemon (376s system / 70s user, 78% memory free) — the churn is
z3-internal (allocator/madvise pattern on this obligation), not memory
pressure, and it did not manifest on Jul 12.  Measuring vampire standalone at
600s to size a possible budget fix; z3's behavior on this obligation is
currently NOT reproducibly under any budget.
--- 15-puzzle obligation: root cause found (environmental) -----------------------
Sampled z3 stacks during the run: the wall budget drains into libsystem_malloc's
xzone allocator making mach_vm_map / mach_vm_reclaim / mach_vm_copy kernel
traps on z3's small-object hot path (smt::enode init / small_object_allocator)
— 220s system vs 48s user at the 300s mark, worsening superlinearly with heap
growth (512s/84s at 900s).  Not memory pressure (78% free, bloop stopped), not
QoS (foreground identical), not malloc env knobs (NanoZone/Xzone/ProbGuard/
SecureAllocator all no-ops), not an OS update (26.4.1 since May), not a
reboot boundary (uptime 8.5 days spans BOTH the Jul 12 pass and the Jul 13
failures).  Conclusion: kernel VM-reclaim state degraded within this boot
under ~100 JVM test cycles + benchmarks; the identical obligation that z3
proved in <240s on Jul 12 now cannot complete in 900s.  Vampire independently
fails at 600s (clause discard = its own memory limit).
The pipeline's BUDGET-EXCEEDED mechanism covers only the egg observational
leg; real smt obligations REQUIRE both provers by design — no code change is
appropriate.  Recommended: reboot, rerun morkl.EquivPipelineTest; if the
allocator pathology persists across reboots, options are (a) single-prover
acceptance for agnostic obligations with the other prover's timeout recorded
(the laws-pipeline STATUS.tsv convention — a policy decision), or (b) running
z3 with an alternative allocator (jemalloc via DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES).
All 5 other pipeline instances (aunt, datalog, gol, temperature, n-queens)
verify green on the current tree; every non-prover gate in the repo is green.

