2026-08-06  Solver timeouts: diagnosed and FIXED; two new certified laws
================================================================================
DIAGNOSIS (dumped the lowered SMT and profiled it rather than guessing):
  nqueens-expanded  141 nodes, 2265 lines, 494 subset + 529 eps edges -> box solve 12.2s
  puzzle3-expanded  297 nodes, 7844 lines, 2505 subset + 2721 eps edges -> box solve TIMEOUT
Not a size problem (141 nodes!). Two causes, both measured:
 1. The emitted subset relation was the full TRANSITIVE CLOSURE. Every edge implied by a two-step
    path is derivable by the solver's own arithmetic, so they were pure redundant rows: 67% of
    puzzle3's constraint lines.
 2. z3's BOX-MODE multi-objective search was the dominant cost -- not the constraint system.
Experiments: transitive reduction alone 494->136 / 2505->218 edges, nqueens 12.2s -> 5.3s.
SPLITTING the three objectives into separate single-objective solves: nqueens 12.2s -> 0.71 + 0.94s;
puzzle3 TIMEOUT -> 1.72 + 13.97s COMPLETE. A REAL (LRA) relaxation was tried and REJECTED: it is
100x faster but loses the upper entirely (max n = oo vs 230) -- integrality is load-bearing there,
so it would have traded the answer for speed.

IMPLEMENTED in SizeZ3:
 * emit the transitive REDUCTION of the ⊑ relation (closure still computed internally, since the
   glb/lub rules and uppersOf/common read it);
 * one solver call per objective, each budgeted separately; a per-objective failure degrades THAT
   endpoint to its baseline instead of losing the whole query;
 * new Status.PartiallySolved(detail) so a partly-established answer is never passed off as
   `Solved` -- found because puzzle15 was reporting "solved" while silently keeping the baseline
   upper. Consumers updated; the gates accept it (it still dominates the baseline).
RESULT: every cornerstone now gets an answer, and puzzle3's upper is genuinely TIGHTENED past the
baseline: 18469 -> 17445. puzzle15 honestly reports PARTIAL(kept baseline for max).

TWO NEW CERTIFIED LAWS (proofs/spatial, both PROVED):
 * sp_head_partition -- a path's head and tail are unique and group h's tail-set is exactly
   {t : cons h t in A}, so the group tail-sets are disjoint and cover: Sigma_g |rest_g| =
   |headed A|. CONSEQUENCE IMPLEMENTED (Lower.sizeBounds + restChainBody): a nested iteration whose
   inner source is the OUTER loop's rest-mention costs ⌈src⌉·⌈innermost body⌉ -- LINEAR in the
   source -- not ⌈src⌉^depth. Guarded by `min` with the old product so it can only tighten.
 * sp_iter_disjoint_cylinders -- bodies keyed by distinct group heads cannot share a path.
Soundness re-gated: SizeBoundsCheck (5000 closed corpus instances), SizeZ3Check, LenBoundsCheck,
SpatialTypeCheck, AlgebraicLaws, CorpusLawValidation, ProductUnionCheck all green.

WHERE THE REMAINING LOOSENESS ACTUALLY IS (honest):
 * nqueens [15, 230] vs truth 32: the z3 optimum EQUALS the baseline, so the search is not the
   problem -- the constraint system holds no more information. The gap is the attack-filter's
   SELECTIVITY (a subtraction removing most candidates), which no cardinality abstraction can bound
   without semantic knowledge of the filter. Not a missing law.
 * The nested-iteration law does NOT move the expanded cornerstones, because EquivPipeline.expand
   unrolls every iteration into ground unions -- there are no Iteration nodes left to bound. It pays
   on UNEXPANDED, annotated programs (the real use case).
 * Expanded unions of ground alternatives (nqueens 230, puzzle3 17445) are loose because the
   alternatives OVERLAP and inclusion-exclusion needs the meets, which are not in the term.
   Computing the overlap would mean folding the literals = evaluation, which is out of bounds.
