================================================================================
2026-08-06  Intermediate-space elimination — driven by abstract interpretation over
            FUNCTIONS with ABSTRACT ANNOTATED INPUTS (the sanctioned route)
================================================================================
Direction settled: removing intermediate spaces is legitimate, but ONLY as a function of abstract
interpretation over functions whose inputs carry abstract annotations. So no separate
partial-evaluation component (the reviewer's suggestion is declined on that basis); instead the
analysis now returns FACTS PLUS A RESIDUAL, which is also review.md item 4.

SpatialTypes.eliminate(body, env) / eliminateIn(routine, env) -> (residual, Vector[Removed]).
Nothing evaluates a subterm. A subterm is deleted when its inferred spatial type is BOTTOM (no class
can hold a path) — derived from the term's syntax, the annotations in `env`, and the certified
relational laws. Deleting it removes the entire computation that produced it; the ordinary Lower laws
then propagate Empty through the parents. Binder envs mirror the analysis exactly (iteration/fold
bind sym to length 1 and rest to the source's tails); a Fixpoint body is rewritten with the recursive
mention UNBOUND (a one-pass rewrite has no accumulated type and assuming one would not be justified);
Call bodies are never touched — they belong to their own routine.

CONTRACT: the residual agrees with the original on every input SATISFYING `env`. Empty env ⇒ the
annotations are vacuous ⇒ unconditional rewrite. With annotations the result is a SPECIALISATION and
is only valid where they hold — the test asserts an annotation-violating input DISAGREES, so the
annotation is provably load-bearing rather than decorative.

STRICTLY STRONGER than the syntactic Lower.SizeEmpty law (`sizeBounds(sp).hi == 0`). The spatial tier
also proves emptiness from
  * LENGTH-DISJOINTNESS: {len 10} ∩ {len 15} — sizeBounds only gets [0,1], SizeEmpty leaves it alone
    (asserted as a precondition in the test so the comparison cannot silently rot);
  * RESTRICTION ANNIHILATION: every path shorter than the shortest prefix;
  * INPUT ANNOTATIONS: `xs : {len 2}` restricted by a length-4 prefix set is empty.

MEASURED (SpatialElimination, 6 tests green):
  * length-disjoint meet buried under a wrap+composition: 6 AST nodes of dead work removed
  * restriction annihilation: whole term to Empty
  * annotated function f(xs : len 2, ys : len 3): both branches provably empty, body collapses to
    Empty under SC.reduce; 40 conforming random inputs agree
  * CORPUS, no annotations at all: fires on 133/1000 programs, 2308 AST nodes removed, 2000
    differential checks clean (unconditional meaning preservation)
  * negative controls: no fact ⇒ no rewrite; Calls left intact; overlapping length classes kept

This is the first consumer of SpatialTypes, so review.md's "no production consumer" is now partly
answered — honestly: it is an OPT-IN entry point, not wired into the default optimizer pipeline, and
the optimizer's own guards still call Lower.sizeBounds. Only the EMPTINESS fact is exploited so far;
the other facts review.md lists (exact prefix, singleton/head set, exact bounded iteration) need the
shape components the domain does not have yet.

