--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THREE SOUNDNESS BUGS FOUND AT INTEGRATION AND FIXED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The adversarial hunter (piece 5, written against the domain by an agent that did not own it) was RED
on first integration: 5 term sweeps + 2 algebra matrices reported 24 minimal witnesses over ~83k
cases. Three distinct root causes, all now fixed and all re-verified:

(a) `SpatialTyping.fixpoint` USED THE UNION TRANSFER AS A LATTICE JOIN.  The concrete operator binds
    the recursive mention to the LAST iterate and returns the UNION of all of them. `Shape.union` is
    the transfer for `A ∪ B`: it keeps the left operand's MUST (sound — `A ∪ B ⊇ A`) and ADDS the
    untracked-head counts. Neither holds for a value drawn from ONE side, so it is not an upper bound
    and the Kleene candidate claimed musts no iterate has. Witness (71 raw cases):
        Singleton("c.b.b").fix(k){TailsIntersection(k)}
        eval    = {b, b.b, c.b.b, ε}
        claimed = shape {b·{b·{ε?}}, c·{b·{b·{ε!}}}}   -- eps=No, so ε was PROVED ABSENT
        and via the product reduction, size.hi = 2 for a 4-path result and len.lo = 2 with ε present
    FIX, in two parts, because there are two obligations and the old code had one:
      * the chain ascends with a real join, `Shape.lub` (MAX of the counts, no lower bound, ε -> May
        unless both agree), over iterates kept MAY-ONLY. May-only is what closes the argument:
        γ_may = γ there, so `Shape.leq` (a γ_may order) is exactly the right certificate AND the body
        is still analysed by the transfers in the STRONG reading they are sound for.
      * the RESULT is an unbounded union of iterates and γ is NOT union-closed ({a} and {b} are both
        in γ({+[0,1] more}), {a,b} is not). What survives is `openCounts` — ε-absence and
        head-set closedness are union-closed, counts are not. So `union(i0, openCounts(c))`.
      `SpatialGamma.lubShape` now DELEGATES to `Shape.lub`: one join, because two spellings of a join
      is how this confusion got in. First attempt at `lub` had its own bug (`lub(∅, b) = b` — ∅ is
      NOT below a must-carrying shape) which the order matrix caught in 9417 raw cases.
(b) `Shape.leq` IGNORED HEADS THE RIGHT SIDE TRACKS AND THE LEFT DOES NOT (129 raw cases).
        leq({ε!, +[1,inf] more}, {ε?, a·{b·{…}}, …, +[0,inf] more}) was accepted,
        yet {a, ε} ∈ γ_may(lhs) and ∉ γ_may(rhs) — rhs's child for `a` has eps=No.
    An untracked head of the left CAN be a key the right tracks, and nothing else looked at that
    pair. FIX: iterate the union of both live key sets (`a.under(h)` already returns ∅ for a closed
    side, so the extra arm is vacuous exactly when it should be). This is load-bearing, not cosmetic:
    `leq` is the fixpoint's convergence test and its certificate.
(c) `SpatialTypes` SUBTRACTION: THE SPILL LOWER BOUND IGNORED THE SUBTRAHEND'S TRACKED CLASSES.
    It discounted only `y.rest.hi`, so a subtrahend whose paths sit in TRACKED classes covered by the
    minuend's spill window removed nothing from `x.rest.lo`. Three shapes of witness:
        (hunt$rec(;$s0) \ $s0)   with $s0={ε}   ->  size.lo 1, DefinitelyNonEmpty, for eval = ∅
        (($s0 ∪ TU(L)) \ TU(L))                ->  size.lo 2                      for eval = ∅
        (Singleton(?p) \ L{17 paths})          ->  size.lo 1                      for eval = ∅
    FIX: the bucket discounts the subtrahend's total over the lengths its window covers (tracked
