KNOWN-OPEN (explicit; none of these is a green test hiding a red fact)
================================================================================
1. THE COST CONSTANTS ARE UNVALIDATED. §4 above. A real gate needs measured per-operator constants
   on both backends, and `cost#(s)` asserted as an upper bound on a counted execution — neither
   exists. ρ = 0.933 over 16 programs is the whole story.
2. HUNT 9's THREE ROWS ARE A NON-LAW, NOT A BUG. `sub`, `raffination` and `tailsInter` consume an
   operand's MUST channel (that is the point of review.md 1) and are therefore sound in the STRONG
   reading ONLY — subtraction is antitone in its right operand, so an operand read may-only licenses
   a LARGER result than sub# returns. gsem_l2_tailsinter_may_unsound PROVES the tailsInter case is
   genuinely unsound as a may law. What makes it safe: the analysis never reads a shape may-only and
   feeds it back into a transfer. It does pass WEAKENED shapes in (Shape.under on an untracked head,
   groupUnion's `rest` binding, fixpoint's iterate) — but γ_s(s) ⊆ γ_s(weaken s), so a weakened shape
   is still a valid STRONG abstraction of the same value, which HUNT 7 covers. If anyone later adds a
   consumer that re-abstracts from the may reading, these three rows become live bugs.
3. THE SEMANTIC PROOFS ARE A MODEL, NOT THE CODE. Bounded depth (1–2), hand-written, no generator
   from obligation to Scala and no mechanical agreement check. The cap arms (MaxDepth/MaxHeads) are
   not in any obligation; `eval`, `Space`, the binders, `Call`, `Fold`, `Fixpoint` appear in none.
   Every code-level claim in this entry rests on the executable suites.
4. `satisfies` IS WEAKER THAN γ AND STAYS SO. It does not verify a per-class LOWER bound for a class
   the value leaves empty (237/38400 non-members accepted; 0 members rejected). Dispatch uses
   `gammaMember`; `satisfies` remains only as the cheap envelope. Certified: gsem_satisfies_weaker.
5. `Shape.leq` IS INCOMPLETE (17.2% of γ-contained pairs undecided). Only costs the Fixpoint chain a
   widening round, but it is why some fixpoints land at ⊤ that need not.
6. FIXPOINT PRECISION AFTER THE FIX. `widenShape` opens every count channel, so a fixpoint that
   reaches the widening loses `headsClosed` and with it the exact head count and a finite size.hi.
   Measured cost of the whole fix on the nested matrix: ⊤ shapes 1556 -> 1557 of 8000, so ~0.01% —
   but I did NOT capture the pre-fix corpus precision counters, so the 936/542/498 numbers in §1 have
   no before-value to compare against. A gentler widening that preserves closedness while still
   terminating is the obvious next precision win.
7. TERMINATION OF THE SHAPE WIDENING IS NOT PROVED. Same status as the histogram (§7 of the design
   doc): structural argument plus an explicit iteration cap (8 rounds, then one widen-and-retry, then
   ⊤). ⊤ always certifies, so the analysis is total, but "it converges" is not a theorem.
8. STILL NOT WIRED INTO ANY PIPELINE. `eliminate`/`eliminateIn` remain the only consumer of the OLD
   tier, and they consume only ⊥. Nothing consumes `Fact`, `headCount`, `BoundedRecursion` or `Cost`
   in `Routine.optimized` or any backend. review.md 8's "one vertical demonstration" is DONE as a
   test (recursion: derive depth 4, residualise, keep the precondition, differential-check) but is
   not DONE as a compiler path.
9. NO PER-NODE TYPED IR (review.md's other half of finding 4). `SpatialTyping.infer` still analyses a
   term and returns one root answer; the shape and histogram each walk the term once (the earlier
   quadratic re-inference is gone) but there is no stable NodeId -> Fact map with provenance, so an
   optimizer still cannot ask "what did you know at this node".
10. `Fold`'s ACCUMULATOR IS STILL OPAQUE. The head-group union is exact, but the accumulator's value
   depends on group ORDER and on `update`, neither of which the domain models; a body that reads the
   accumulator degrades exactly where it reads it. review.md 2's complaint about Fold is only
   partly answered.

