6. WHAT REMAINS OPEN AFTER THIS INCREMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. COST TIGHTNESS ON THE CORNERSTONES IS THE BIGGEST HONEST GAP. 3,962,335x on trie/zipper `Touch`,
   213,465x on `Alloc`. Containment at 100% says nothing about usefulness at that slack. The two
   structural fixes named by the measurement are (a) a fixpoint over the size lattice / an
   interprocedural size summary for datalog-sn, and (b) bounding a rest-chained nest by the PREFIX
   PROFILE (Sum of K_d) instead of the per-level product for puzzle15 -- raising SpatialCost.MaxDepth
   was TRIED and saturates to inf because a 16-level product overflows the algebra's Long.
2. THE LAW CHANNEL HAS NO BOMB-SUBTERM TEST OF ITS OWN. Nothing prevents a CALLER-supplied law from
   calling `eval` inside its `bound`; for user laws the no-evaluation invariant is a contract, not a
   check. The shipped laws are covered indirectly (SpatialAcceptance 6a-6d run the law-refined
   pipeline inside `noEval`), but a sentinel on the channel itself is open work.
3. NO LAW IS SMT-PROVED. Every law in the library is `ExecutableChecked` by exhaustive finite search
   (512 digraphs; 512 Life fields; exact n-queens counts n<=8). `SmtProved` and `Assumed` exist as
   labels with no current occupant in the shipped library. Nothing generates a Scala law from a
   proof obligation.
4. THE PRODUCT ORDER'S RESIDUAL INCOMPLETENESS IS STRUCTURAL, not a missing clause: 52 of 202
   measured false negatives are containments visible only to the conjunction of shape and histogram.
   Closing them needs a non-componentwise order (or a shape-aware histogram clause), which is a
   design change, not a repair.
5. THE REDUCER STILL HAS NO SMT OBLIGATION AND NO TERMINATION PROOF (carried forward unchanged).
6. Shape.MaxDepth/MaxHeads remain carrier-wide vals, so `shapeDepth`/`shapeWidth` narrow only.
   Threading a config through Shape's constructors is still the fix, and it is still not done.
7. The two red suites in section 5.

